## MCP (Model Context Protocol) configuration

MCP servers extend Claude Code with external tools, databases, APIs.
They run as separate processes that Claude talks to over JSON-RPC.
See <https://code.claude.com/docs/en/mcp> for the canonical reference.

### Scope (where MCP servers are configured)

| Scope | Location | Shared with |
|---|---|---|
| **Managed** | `managed-settings.json` mcpServers | All users on the machine |
| **User** | `~/.claude/settings.json` mcpServers | All projects, just you |
| **Project** | `<project>/.mcp.json` | Anyone who checks out the repo |
| **Local** | `<project>/.claude/settings.local.json` | Just you in this project |

Cited: <https://code.claude.com/docs/en/mcp>. Add via
`claude mcp add <name> <command> [args...]`.

### Security rules

These are non-negotiable. An MCP server gets the same access the
user has.

- **Vet the source.** Prefer servers listed in the
  [Anthropic Directory](https://claude.ai/directory) or published by
  the `@modelcontextprotocol` org on npm. Anything else, read the
  code.
- **Pin by version.** Don't use `latest` or unpinned `@X.Y` ranges
  in production `.mcp.json`. Pin to an exact version + sha when
  available.
- **No secrets in `.mcp.json`.** That file usually gets committed.
  Pass credentials via env vars; reference them with
  `"${ENV_VAR_NAME}"` syntax where supported, or via the user-level
  `~/.claude/settings.json`.
- **Project-level `.mcp.json` is third-party code.** Review every
  PR that touches it the same way you'd review a new npm
  dependency. A typo-squat MCP server is the next vector.
- **Sandbox where possible.** Filesystem MCP servers should be
  scoped to a single directory subtree, not `/`. Database MCP
  servers should connect with a least-privilege user.
- **Watch the elicitation events.** The `Elicitation` /
  `ElicitationResult` hook events fire when an MCP server asks the
  user for input. Audit-log them in security-sensitive projects.

### Safe-to-trust starter list

These are commonly recommended and published by the official org.
Pin to a real version when you adopt them; the strings below are
illustrative.

- `@modelcontextprotocol/server-filesystem`: exposes a directory
  subtree. Scope it tightly.
- `@modelcontextprotocol/server-git`: git-repo introspection. Read
  only by default.
- `@modelcontextprotocol/server-fetch`: HTTP GET / POST. Useful;
  also a potential SSRF vector if you let it hit internal URLs.
- `@modelcontextprotocol/server-sqlite`: local SQLite query.
- `@modelcontextprotocol/server-postgres`: Postgres query. Use a
  read-only DB user.

### Configuration patterns

**User-level**, in `~/.claude/settings.json`:

```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "filesystem-home": {
      "command": "npx",
      "args": ["-y", "@modelcontextprotocol/server-filesystem", "/Users/me/projects"]
    }
  }
}
```

**Project-level**, in `<project>/.mcp.json`:

```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "github": {
      "command": "npx",
      "args": ["-y", "@modelcontextprotocol/server-github"],
      "env": {
        "GITHUB_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN": "${GITHUB_PAT}"
      }
    }
  }
}
```

The first time Claude Code encounters a project `.mcp.json`, it
shows an approval dialog. Decline if the server isn't in your
allowlist. Approval persists per-project.

### Auditing

Run `/audit-mcp-config` to enumerate every configured MCP server
+ flag risky patterns (unpinned versions, secrets in repo file,
overly-broad filesystem scope).
